From c48df9848bc04ce032f391e018d1b3b8a1f6815d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 06:34:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 13/16] workers: report the host's CPU and memory, and show
 them to watchers

Slot use says how full a builder's slots are, not how busy its machine
is. A pool whose slots look 60 to 90% full while its CPUs sit at 30%
looks healthy, and nothing on the stream says otherwise.

Builders read their host once a second and keep the last minute of its
CPU times. The CPU figures are averages over that minute, in thousandths
of a CPU: CPUs busy, and CPUs idle with I/O of theirs outstanding. A
builder that waits on its disk shows as such, not as idle capacity. All
of it is host-wide, so any other process on the host counts too.

On Linux they read /proc/stat and /proc/meminfo. Busy is user, system
and interrupt time, steal, the time a hypervisor took, counts as
neither, and memory in use is MemTotal minus MemAvailable. On macOS they
read the Mach host statistics: busy is user, system and nice time, and
memory in use is active, wired and compressed pages, which approximates
"Memory Used" in Activity Monitor. macOS has no iowait, so its builders
send none rather than 0. The arithmetic and the window are shared, and
only the read is per platform.

WorkerReport carries them in a session's first report, then when busy
moved by at least 5% of the CPUs, memory in use by 5% of the total, or
30 s passed since they last went out. Like the rest of the report, they
go only to a scheduler that asked with ReportsWanted. Other hosts send
none.

The scheduler keeps a builder's last values, with busy and iowait, where
sent, capped at its CPUs and memory in use at its total. WorkerState
gains cpu_busy_millicores, cpu_iowait_millicores, cpu_cores,
mem_used_bytes and mem_total_bytes. They go out with the report-driven
WorkerStates, at most once a second per builder. Hello clears them until
the session's first report, so a builder that sends none shows none.

substituter-tally-test covers the /proc parsing, the conversion of
macOS's counters, the window, the real host's figures and when reports
carry the load. The live macOS read runs only on macOS. events-test
covers what watchers get.
